    "title": "STMicro Eyes $1.5 Billion Convertible Bonds After AI-Fueled Jump",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "(Bloomberg) -- STMicroelectronics NV is looking to raise $1.5 billion from selling debt that can be exchanged into equity after the chipmaker's shares tripled in value so far this year.\nMost Read from Bloomberg\n-\nIran's Deputy Foreign Minister Confirms Deal Reached With US\n-\nUS and Iran Agree to Halt War, Restart Middle East Oil Shipments\nSTMicro is offering the convertible bonds — debt that can be swapped for shares at a pre-agreed price — across two tranches, one due in 2031 and the other in 2033. The proceeds will be put toward the early redemption of $750 million zero-coupon notes maturing in 2027, with the remainder used for general corporate purposes, the company said in a statement.\nSTMicro shares fell about 3% as of 9:42 a.m. in Paris trading. The stock has surged almost 200% so far this year, making it the third-best performer in the Stoxx Europe 600 gauge, amid booming demand for data center chips.\nThe French-Italian company, which counts Tesla Inc., Apple Inc. and Amazon.com Inc.'s AWS among customers, has expanded beyond consumer electronics and automotive chips into higher growth areas including data centers. Last month, it said revenue from that business will reach $1 billion this year, fueled by demand for artificial intelligence infrastructure, up from a previous forecast of more than $500 million.\nThe recent rally also means that the company's share price is well above the conversion price on the 2027 notes of $45.10. Refinancing that bond removes the risk of share dilution, as investors are more likely to want to swap their debt into shares.\nThe tranche of convertibles due 2031 will have a fixed coupon of between 0% and 0.5%, and the conversion premium will be set between 47.5% and 52.5% above the company's reference share price. The notes due 2033 will have a fixed coupon of between 0.625% and 1.125% and the conversion premium will be set between 50% and 55%.\nSTMicro is among a swathe of AI-related companies selling convertible bonds in recent months as issuers look for the cheapest way to fund investments such as data centers. Last month, network equipment supplier Ciena Corp. proposed a $2 billion offering of convertible senior notes, while AI firms Nebius Group NV and Oracle Corp. have also both done large convertible bond deals.\nBNP Paribas SA and JPMorgan Chase & Co. are helping to arrange STMicro's offering.",
